#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148435} :activity/id #uuid "c33fcd20-192a-525c-a003-08244d7958fe" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049950} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148435 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148757 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will learn how to use Laplace transforms to solve second-order ODEs. As before, let's practice finding the Laplace transform of a second-order differential equation first.\n\nFor example, let's find the Laplace transform of $y(t)$ if \n\n$$\ny''  =  t, \\qquad y(0)=2, \\qquad y'(0)=3.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the Laplace transform of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ y'' \\right\\} =  \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ t \\right\\}\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify each side.\n\n- For the left-hand side, we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a second derivative. Letting $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ y \\right\\}=Y(s)$ and using the initial conditions $y(0)=2, \\, y'(0)=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{''}} & ={s}^{2}L{y}-sy(0)-{y}^{'}(0) \\\\  & ={s}^{2}Y(s)-sy(0)-{y}^{'}(0) \\\\  & ={s}^{2}Y(s)-2s-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the right-hand side, we use the following result from the table of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\{  t  \\} = \\dfrac{1}{s^2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $Y(s){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{''}} & =L{t} \\\\ {s}^{2}Y(s)-2s-3 & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ {s}^{2}Y(s) & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+3+2s \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{4}}+\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $y(t)$ is $Y(s) =  \\dfrac{1}{s^4} + \\dfrac{3}{s^2} + \\dfrac{2}{s}.$\n\nLet's see more examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "d4b52997-ca96-5bc0-845a-b2943def6e25" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4113 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "19d3669b-0462-5b59-8a2f-88a325af813e" :step/math-academy-id 604719 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148436}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148436 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147516 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147364 :question/math-academy-id "e-6663"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4487c2b9-b681-5e1d-a2d9-c3231afcf03d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048605} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050677}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351494} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377621} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377886} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186607581}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Laplace Transform of a Second-Order ODE"} :step/id #uuid "04d715c4-570a-53d3-be33-0a3fbcda94ed" :step/math-academy-id 604720 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148437}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148437 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148758 :tutorial/content "Now, let's see how to use Laplace transforms to solve second-order ODEs. There are two steps:\n\n- **Step 1:** Find the Laplace transform of the equation, and solve for $Y(s)$.\n\n- **Step 2:** Find the solution by taking the inverse Laplace transform.\n\nTo illustrate, let's continue solving the following differential equation:\n\n$$\ny''  =  t, \\quad y(0)=2, \\quad y'(0)=3\n$$\n\nEarlier, we found that the Laplace transform of $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\nY(s) =  \\dfrac{1}{s^4} + \\dfrac{3}{s^2} + \\dfrac{2}{s}.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to take the inverse Laplace transform. From the table of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}} & ={t}^{3}, \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}} & =t, \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, taking the inverse Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{Y(s)} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{{s}^{4}}+\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{s}} \\\\ y(t) & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{{s}^{4}}}+{L}^{-1}{\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}}}+{L}^{-1}{\\frac{2}{s}} \\\\ y(t) & =\\frac{1}{6}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}}+3{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}}+2{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}} \\\\ y(t) & =\\frac{1}{6}{t}^{3}+3t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the ODE is $y(t) = \\dfrac{1}{6} t^3 + 3 t + 2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1d2f1d1a-f65f-58b0-81e3-b67a89dcee71" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4114 :tutorial/title "Using Laplace Transforms to Solve Second-Order Differential Equations"} :step/id #uuid "b201edef-c1ff-5f79-848c-4e3e546b3a32" :step/math-academy-id 604724 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148438}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148438 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147517 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147365 :question/math-academy-id "e-6666"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30d5c275-af4a-5bdd-a97c-c937824d8e94" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147794} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186593257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186596697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186607639}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Initial Value Problem Using Laplace Transforms"} :step/id #uuid "26d61a91-facd-5261-adc5-a1ee7c1d2edb" :step/math-academy-id 604725}] :activity/title "Solving Second-Order ODEs Using Laplace Transforms" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147719}]}
